  "text": "Five quick ones. Reach for a hash map for counting, grouping and have-I-seen-this, in constant time on average. Counting labels gives the majority class, the baseline any model has to beat: here, cat, half the time. To deduplicate a list and keep its order, check a set, and append to a list, or use dict from keys, in one line. Both are linear. Sorting first is n log n, and it loses the order. To decode classes from a probability matrix, n rows by k classes, take arg max along axis one: one class per row. Along axis zero you get k answers instead, so check the shape. Accuracy, precision and recall from scratch are boolean masks, summed, with no loop. Handle the empty case: a model that predicts no positives has a precision of zero over zero. NumPy returns not a number; scikit-learn reports zero, with a warning. And k-nearest neighbors. The n distances cost n times d. Then a full sort costs n log n, or a heap of size k, n log k. Either way, computing the distances costs more than choosing among them, so repeated queries need an index: a KD-tree, a ball tree, or an approximate one like HNSW. Code gives you a number. Statistics says how far to trust it. Next: statistics and probability.",
